e0$. By [F10] for the finitely presented $Q_c$-module $B$, $E_{\mathfrak q}\cong\operatorname{Hom}_{(Q_c)_{\mathfrak q}} (B_{\mathfrak q},(Q_c)_{\mathfrak q})$. Now $B_{\mathfrak q}$ is a nonzero finite module over the local ring $(Q_c)_{\mathfrak q}$ of dimension zero, because $\mathfrak q$ is minimal over $I/J$; it is Cohen--Macaulay of dimension $0$ over that ring by [F11] applied to $B$ over $Q_c$, so it has finite length. A nonzero finite-length module over a local ring admits a surjection onto the residue field, and the residue field of $(Q_c)_{\mathfrak q}$ maps injectively into $(Q_c)_{\mathfrak q}$ as the image of the natural map; composing gives a nonzero element of $\operatorname{Hom}_{(Q_c)_{\mathfrak q}}(B_{\mathfrak q}, (Q_c)_{\mathfrak q})$, so $E_{\mathfrak q}
e0$. For a finite module the support is the closed set $V(\operatorname{ann}E)$, so $\mathfrak q\subseteq\mathfrak p$ gives $E_{\mathfrak p}
e0$ as well. Hence $\operatorname{Supp}_B(E)=V(I)=\operatorname{Supp}_B(B)$. [F10, F11, step 4.1]
